- Have been convicted of an offence involving moral turpitude (as per the Central Government’s view);  
- Are physically or mentally incapable of acting as a Member;  
- Have a financial or other interest likely to prejudice their functions; or  
- Have abused their position such that their continuance would be prejudicial to public interest.  

[Section 21(1)]

full.answer:
A person is disqualified from being appointed or continuing as a Member of the Board if she:

- Has been adjudged an insolvent;  
- Has been convicted of an offence that the Central Government considers to involve moral turpitude;  
- Is physically or mentally incapable of acting as a Member;  
- Holds a financial or other interest likely to prejudice her functions; or  
- Has abused her position such that her continuance would be prejudicial to public interest.  

[Section 21(1)]

The Board may deal with a false or frivolous complaint by:

- Issuing a warning to the complainant;  
- Imposing costs on the complainant;  
- Closing the proceedings if it finds the complaint lacks merit.  

[Section 28(12)] – Board may issue a warning or impose costs.  
[Section 28(4)] – Board may close proceedings for insufficient grounds.
